course: dictionary
lesson: 9090
title: Words with two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sluice is a gate that controls how much water can flow through. Think of it as a door for water: open it a little and a trickle gets through, open it wide and a lot rushes past. You can find sluices on rivers, canals and mill streams.
explain: Sluice can also be a verb, an action word. «Farmers sluice out the cowshed» means they wash it with lots of water. «Rain sluices down the street» means the rain pours fast along it. Here the word shows water moving in a strong, rushing way.
explain: To decide which job the word does, check the sentence. The lock example says «small sluices in the top gates let water in», so the sluices are things that let water through. Ask: is it a thing or an action? If a thing is doing something to water, sluice is probably a noun. If someone or something is doing an action, it is probably a verb.
fact: A sluice is a gate or channel that controls how much water flows.
fact: Sluices can be on a river, a canal or a mill stream, and are sometimes called sluice gates.
fact: To sluice means to wash something with lots of water, as in «sluice down the yard».
fact: Water can also sluice, which means it pours or rushes out.
fact: Sluices help control floods and water levels, and they can turn mill wheels.
exercise: «The sluice gate was opened a little, so only a trickle of water got through.» What is a sluice? | *A gate or channel that controls how much water flows | A kind of small boat | A type of bridge over a river
exercise: «To take a boat up through a lock, small sluices in the top gates let water in.» What do the sluices do here? | They keep the gates shut for ever | They lift the boat out of the water | They turn the boat around | *They let water in
exercise: «Farmers sluice out the cowshed.» What does sluice mean here? | To hide something under straw | To close a gate tight | To paint the walls | *To wash with lots of water
test: «Rain sluices down the street.» In this sentence, sluice is a verb meaning what? | To count the drops | To keep dry under cover | To dig a hole | *To pour or rush out
test: Which sentence uses sluice as a noun, a thing? | We sluice the cups after tea | The farmer will sluice the yard tomorrow | *The river has a sluice that holds back the flood | Water sluiced out of the pipe
exam: Ben reads «The sluices were shut to stop the flood.» Is sluice a thing or an action here? | *A thing: a gate that controls water | A feeling of worry | A colour of the river | An action: to wash something with water
